  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "The number \\(2^{48}-1\\) is exactly divisible by two numbers between \\(60\\) and \\(70.\\) These numbers are:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(61,\\) \\(63\\)",
    "B": "\\(61,\\) \\(65\\)",
    "C": "\\(63,\\) \\(65\\)",
    "D": "\\(63,\\) \\(67\\)",
    "E": "\\(67,\\) \\(69\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The requested numbers are near \\(64=2^6\\), suggesting the factors \\(2^6-1\\) and \\(2^6+1\\). Repeatedly applying the difference-of-squares identity reveals both:\n\\[2^{48}-1=(2^{24}-1)(2^{24}+1)\\]\n\\[=(2^{12}-1)(2^{12}+1)(2^{24}+1)\\]\n\\[=(2^6-1)(2^6+1)(2^{12}+1)(2^{24}+1).\\]\nThus \\(63=2^6-1\\) and \\(65=2^6+1\\) each divide \\(2^{48}-1\\), because each appears as a factor in an integer product.\n\nBoth are between \\(60\\) and \\(70\\). The question states that exactly two numbers in this interval divide the given integer, so these two distinct divisors are the required pair: \\(\\boxed{63,65}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
